---
import DocsLayout from '../../layouts/DocsLayout.astro';
import CodeBlock from '../../components/CodeBlock.astro';
import CommandOutput from '../../components/CommandOutput.astro';
---

<DocsLayout title="CLI identifiers" activeNav="/cli/identifiers">
  <h1>Identifiers</h1>
  <p>
    An identifier is what an app is called on one platform: the iOS bundle ID, the Android
    package name, or the web domain. Builds, build profiles, and credentials all attach to an
    identifier rather than to the app directly, so an app that ships on two platforms has two
    identifiers and two independent signing setups.
  </p>

  <h2>Commands</h2>
  <CodeBlock lang="bash" code={`appmctl identifiers list   [--app APP] [--per-page N]
appmctl identifiers show   [--app APP] IDENTIFIER_ID
appmctl identifiers create [--app APP] --platform ios|android|web --identifier ID \\
                           [--name NAME] [--version VERSION] [--version-code N]
appmctl identifiers update [--app APP] IDENTIFIER_ID [--platform PLATFORM] [--identifier ID] \\
                           [--name NAME] [--version VERSION] [--version-code N]
appmctl identifiers delete [--app APP] IDENTIFIER_ID`} />

  <h3>List</h3>
  <CommandOutput command="appmctl identifiers list" capture="identifiers-list" />

  <h3>Show one identifier</h3>
  <CommandOutput
    command="appmctl identifiers show 019fc789-479a-71b2-800a-1b8c4aed6fc7"
    capture="identifiers-show"
  />

  <h2>Fields</h2>
  <table>
    <thead>
      <tr><th>Flag</th><th>Applies to</th><th>Notes</th></tr>
    </thead>
    <tbody>
      <tr><td><code>--platform</code></td><td>all</td><td><code>ios</code>, <code>android</code>, or <code>web</code>. Required on create.</td></tr>
      <tr><td><code>--identifier</code></td><td>all</td><td>Bundle ID, package name, or domain. Required on create.</td></tr>
      <tr><td><code>--name</code></td><td>all</td><td>Display name, shown in the UI and in build listings</td></tr>
      <tr><td><code>--version</code></td><td>all</td><td>Marketing version, e.g. <code>1.4.0</code></td></tr>
      <tr><td><code>--version-code</code></td><td>android only</td><td>Integer build number. The API rejects it for iOS and web.</td></tr>
    </tbody>
  </table>

  <h3>Format rules</h3>
  <p>
    For <code>ios</code> and <code>android</code> the identifier must be reverse-DNS: at
    least two segments separated by <code>.</code>, <code>_</code>, or <code>-</code>, with
    alphanumeric characters inside each segment. For <code>web</code> it must be a valid
    domain name, and it is lowercased before validation. An identifier is unique per app and
    platform, so the same bundle ID can exist on two different apps but not twice on one.
  </p>
  <p>
    The CLI checks only the platform value locally. Everything else — the format, uniqueness,
    the Android-only <code>version_code</code> rule — is enforced by the API, which keeps the
    CLI from drifting away from the server's rules as they change.
  </p>

  <h3>Create</h3>
  <CodeBlock lang="bash" code={`# Android, with a version code
appmctl identifiers create --platform android --identifier com.acme.customer \\
  --name 'Customer App (Android)' --version 1.4.0 --version-code 1408

# iOS
appmctl identifiers create --platform ios --identifier com.acme.customer \\
  --name 'Customer App (iOS)' --version 1.4.0

# Web
appmctl identifiers create --platform web --identifier customer.acme.example \\
  --name 'Customer Web'`} />
  <p>Inspect the request body before sending if you are scripting this:</p>
  <CodeBlock lang="bash" code={`appmctl --dry-run identifiers create --platform android \\
  --identifier com.acme.customer --version-code 1408

# dry-run: no request sent
# method: POST
# url: https://manager.example.com/api/v1/acme-mobile/apps/customer-app/identifiers
# body: {
#   "platform": "android",
#   "identifier": "com.acme.customer",
#   "version_code": 1408
# }`} />

  <h3>Update</h3>
  <p>
    <code>update</code> is partial: send only what changes. It requires at least one field.
    Bumping a version before a release is the common case:
  </p>
  <CodeBlock lang="bash" code={`appmctl identifiers update 019fc789-479a-71b2-800a-1b8c4aed6fc7 \\
  --version 1.5.0 --version-code 1500`} />
  <p>
    Changing an identifier's platform to <code>web</code> is refused while credentials or
    build profiles are still attached, since web identifiers cannot hold signing material.
    Detach those first.
  </p>

  <h3>Delete</h3>
  <CodeBlock lang="bash" code={`appmctl identifiers delete 019fc789-479a-71b2-800a-1b8c4aed6fc7`} />

  <h2>Expo identifiers</h2>
  <p>
    When an app is linked to an Expo project, <code>appmctl eas identifiers</code> shows the
    same identifiers alongside their sync state on Expo, and
    <code>eas sync-identifiers</code> pushes local ones upward. See
    <a href="/cli/integrations/eas">EAS</a>.
  </p>

  <h2>Scopes</h2>
  <p>
    <code>identifiers:read</code> for <code>list</code> and <code>show</code>,
    <code>identifiers:write</code> plus the <code>manage_applications</code> team permission
    for <code>create</code>, <code>update</code>, and <code>delete</code>.
  </p>
</DocsLayout>
